# Line endings
#
# One canonical form in the repository, LF, regardless of who committed from
# where. Without this, a Windows checkout rewrites files on commit and every
# `git add` prints a wall of "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings — and a
# formatter that asserts byte-exact output has to keep normalizing around it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files whose content is bytes, not text.
*.wasm binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.vsix binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Language statistics
#
# GitHub computes the language bar with Linguist, which counts every tracked
# file it recognizes unless told otherwise. Three things distort it here, and
# all three are marked rather than deleted, because each file is legitimately
# committed:
#
#   * playground/index.html is 131 KB of GENERATED bundle — the whole engine
#     inlined by playground/build.mjs. Left unmarked it is the single largest
#     file in the repository and reports the project as substantially "HTML",
#     which describes the build output rather than anything anyone wrote.
#   * llms.txt / llms-full.txt are the documentation re-emitted for machines.
#   * The conformance corpus and the error index are generated from source.
#
# `linguist-generated` also collapses these in diffs, which is what you want:
# a 620-case corpus regeneration should not bury a one-line source change in
# review.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

playground/index.html linguist-generated=true
llms.txt linguist-generated=true
llms-full.txt linguist-generated=true
docs/reference/errors.md linguist-generated=true
conformance/cases/*.json linguist-generated=true
conformance/manifest.json linguist-generated=true

# Vendored grammar output, when it is built locally.
tree-sitter-orbit/src/** linguist-generated=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .orbit files
#
# These currently render on GitHub as plain text and count toward nothing,
# because Linguist does not know the extension. That is not something a
# repository can configure: `linguist-language` can only map a file to a
# language Linguist ALREADY defines, so the options are to be listed upstream
# or to be nothing.
#
# Mapping `.orbit` to a neighbouring language to borrow its highlighting was
# considered and rejected. It would report Orbit as Liquid — a language it is
# deliberately not compatible with — in exchange for approximate colours.
#
# The real fix is the upstream submission prepared in linguist/, which is
# gated on adoption rather than on engineering: Linguist requires a language to
# be in use across a few hundred repositories before it will accept it.
# Marking the intent here so the next person does not re-derive it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

*.orbit text eol=lf linguist-detectable=true
